            <ArticleTitle>Non-opioid analgesics: Novel approaches to perioperative analgesia for major spine surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>79-89</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bpa.2015.11.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1521-6896(15)00073-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Perioperative pain management is a significant challenge following major spine surgery. Many pathways contribute to perioperative pain, including nociceptive, inflammatory, and neuropathic sources. Although opioids have long been a mainstay for perioperative analgesia, other non-opioid therapies have been increasingly used as part of a multimodal analgesic regimen to provide improved pain control while minimizing opioid-related side effects. Here we review the evidence supporting the use of novel analgesic approaches as an alternative to intravenous opioids for major spine surgery.</AbstractText>
